\label{ch:graph-ir}

Graph-based intermediate representations abandon the linear instruction sequence in favor of an unordered graph where nodes represent operations and edges represent data and control dependencies. The most prominent variant is the \emph{sea of nodes}, where all operations---including control flow---float freely in a graph, scheduled only when needed. This chapter develops the theory and practice of graph-based IRs, from value graphs to full sea-of-nodes implementations.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 57.1
%% ============================================================
\section{Value Graphs and Data-Flow Graphs}\label{sec:value-graphs}

A \emph{value graph} (or \emph{data-flow graph}) represents a program as a directed graph where nodes are operations and edges carry values.

\subsection{Structure}\label{sec:value-graph-structure}

\begin{definition}[Value Graph]\label{def:value-graph}
A \emph{value graph} is a directed graph $G = (N, E)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Each node $n \in N$ represents an operation (addition, load, call, etc.) or a constant.
  \item Each edge $(u, v) \in E$ represents a data dependency: $v$ uses the result of $u$.
  \item Each edge is labeled with an operand position (first argument, second argument, etc.).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Source:
  x = a + b
  y = x * 2
  z = y - a

  // Value graph:
  // a ----\
  //        +---> [x: +] ---> [y: *] ---> [z: -]
  // b ----/          |          |             ^
  //                  |    2 ---/              |
  //                  |                        |
  //                  +---- a ----------------/
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Advantages over Linear IR}\label{sec:value-graph-advantages}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Explicit dependencies.} Data dependencies are represented as edges, not implicit in instruction ordering.
  \item \textbf{Common subexpression elimination.} Identical operations can be merged (shared nodes).
  \item \textbf{Dead code elimination.} Nodes with no consumers (except side-effecting nodes) can be removed.
  \item \textbf{Reordering freedom.} Operations can be reordered freely as long as dependencies are respected.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Limitations}\label{sec:value-graph-limits}

Pure value graphs cannot represent:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Control flow.} Conditional branches, loops, and exceptions require additional structure.
  \item \textbf{Side effects.} Operations with side effects (stores, calls) require ordering constraints.
  \item \textbf{Memory.} Memory operations require alias information to determine dependencies.
\end{itemize}

