%% Section 57.2
%% ============================================================
\section{Sea of Nodes Representation}\label{sec:sea-of-nodes}

The \emph{sea of nodes} extends value graphs to include control flow and side effects in a single graph.

\subsection{Core Idea}\label{sec:sea-core}

In a sea of nodes, \emph{all} operations are nodes in a single graph. There is no distinction between ``instructions'' and ``control flow''---both are nodes connected by edges.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Data nodes.} Arithmetic operations, loads, constants.
  \item \textbf{Control nodes.} If, loop, return, region (merge point).
  \item \textbf{Phi nodes.} Merge data values at control-flow join points.
  \item \textbf{Projection nodes.} Extract results from multi-output operations (e.g., the value from a load that also produces a memory state).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Control and Data Edges}\label{sec:control-data-edges}

The sea of nodes uses two kinds of edges:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Data edges.} Represent value dependencies. A data edge from $u$ to $v$ means $v$ uses the value produced by $u$.
  \item \textbf{Control edges.} Represent control dependencies. A control edge from $u$ to $v$ means $v$ executes after $u$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Source:
  if (cond) {
    x = a + b;
  } else {
    x = a - b;
  }
  return x;

  // Sea of nodes:
  // [Start] --control--> [If: cond]
  // [If] --true--> [TrueRegion]
  // [If] --false--> [FalseRegion]
  // [TrueRegion] --control--> [Add: a, b]
  // [FalseRegion] --control--> [Sub: a, b]
  // [TrueRegion] --data--> [Phi]
  // [FalseRegion] --data--> [Phi]
  // [Phi] --data--> [Return]
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Regions and Phi Nodes}\label{sec:regions-phi}

\emph{Region} nodes represent control-flow merge points (analogous to basic blocks in a CFG). \emph{Phi} nodes select data values based on which control path was taken.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Region node: merges control from two predecessors
  Region(pred1, pred2)

  // Phi node: selects data based on control path
  Phi(region, value_from_pred1, value_from_pred2)
\end{verbatim}

A phi node is associated with a region node. When control arrives at the region from predecessor $i$, the phi node produces the value from predecessor $i$.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 57.3
%% ============================================================
\section{Phi Nodes in Sea of Nodes}\label{sec:phi-sea}

In the sea of nodes, phi nodes are first-class nodes, just like arithmetic operations.

\subsection{Phi Node Semantics}\label{sec:phi-sea-sem}

A phi node $p = \phi(r, v_1, v_2)$ is associated with a region $r$ that has two control inputs. If the region was entered from its first control input, $p$ produces $v_1$; if from the second, $p$ produces $v_2$.

\subsection{Phi Node Optimization}\label{sec:phi-sea-opt}

Phi nodes in the sea of nodes can be optimized:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Phi identity.} If all data inputs are the same, the phi node is replaced by that value.
  \item \textbf{Phi cycle.} If a phi node references itself (through a loop), it represents a loop-carried value.
  \item \textbf{Phi hoisting.} If a phi node's inputs are loop-invariant, the phi can be moved out of the loop.
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================================
%% Section 57.4
%% ============================================================
\section{Scheduling from Unordered Graphs}\label{sec:scheduling}

The sea of nodes is unordered: operations can be scheduled in any order that respects dependencies. \emph{Scheduling} assigns each node to a position in a linear instruction sequence.

\subsection{Global Code Motion}\label{sec:global-code-motion}

Click's \emph{global code motion} algorithm~\cite{click1995} schedules nodes by:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Earliest placement.} Move each node as early as possible (to the beginning of its earliest dominating region).
  \item \textbf{Latest placement.} Move each node as late as possible (to the end of its latest dominated region, just before its first use).
  \item \textbf{Block assignment.} Assign each node to a basic block based on its scheduled position.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{List Scheduling}\label{sec:list-scheduling}

\emph{List scheduling} is a simpler approach:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Start from the entry node.
  \item At each step, select a node whose dependencies are all satisfied.
  \item Use a heuristic (e.g., longest path to exit) to break ties.
  \item Emit the node and update the set of ready nodes.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Scheduling with Resource Constraints}\label{sec:scheduling-resources}

Real processors have resource constraints (limited functional units, instruction issue width). Scheduling must respect these constraints:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Latency.} Operations have different latencies; the scheduler must respect them.
  \item \textbf{Throughput.} The processor can issue only a limited number of instructions per cycle.
  \item \textbf{Functional units.} Different operations require different functional units (ALU, FPU, load/store).
\end{itemize}

