%% Section 57.5
%% ============================================================
\section{Advantages for Optimization}\label{sec:sea-advantages}

The sea of nodes offers several advantages for optimization:

\subsection{Global Value Numbering}\label{sec:gvn}

\emph{Global value numbering} (GVN) identifies computations that produce the same value, even across basic blocks. In the sea of nodes, this is natural: two nodes with the same operation and the same inputs are the same computation and can be merged.

\subsection{Redundant Load Elimination}\label{sec:load-elimination}

If a load is dominated by another load with the same address and no intervening store, the second load can be replaced by the first. In the sea of nodes, this is a simple pattern match on the graph.

\subsection{Partial Redundancy Elimination}\label{sec:pre}

\emph{Partial redundancy elimination} (PRE) moves computations to avoid redundant execution. In the sea of nodes, PRE is implemented by \emph{lazy code motion}: operations float to their optimal position in the graph.

\subsection{Speculative Optimization}\label{sec:speculative}

The sea of nodes supports speculative optimization: operations can be moved speculatively (before the check that makes them safe) and the check can be used to deoptimize if the speculation fails.

\begin{insight}
The sea of nodes' key insight is that the distinction between ``instructions'' and ``control flow'' is an artifact of linear representations. By representing everything as nodes in a graph, the optimizer can reason about the program's semantics without being constrained by the instruction ordering. The cost is complexity: the graph must be maintained carefully, and scheduling must be performed before code generation.
\end{insight}

