\section{Implementations: Clip, Graal, V8}\label{sec:sea-implementations}

\subsection{Clip (Click's Compiler)}\label{sec:clip}

Cliff Click's Clip compiler~\cite{click1995} was the first to use a sea-of-nodes IR for a Java compiler. It demonstrated that the sea of nodes enables aggressive global optimizations, including GVN and PRE, that are difficult to implement in linear IRs.

\subsection{Graal}\label{sec:graal}

Graal (now GraalVM) uses a sea-of-nodes IR called the \emph{Graal IR}. Key features:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Floating nodes.} Most operations float freely in the graph.
  \item \textbf{Fixed nodes.} Side-effecting operations (stores, calls) are fixed to specific positions.
  \item \textbf{Guard nodes.} Speculative checks (type checks, null checks) are represented as guard nodes that can be replaced by deoptimization.
  \item \textbf{Partial escape analysis.} Objects that do not escape the method can be allocated on the stack or scalar-replaced.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{V8 TurboFan}\label{sec:turbofan}

V8's TurboFan optimizing compiler uses a sea-of-nodes IR for JavaScript optimization. Key features:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Speculative inlining.} Function calls are inlined speculatively, with deoptimization if the callee changes.
  \item \textbf{Hidden class optimization.} Object property accesses are optimized based on the object's hidden class (shape).
  \item \textbf{Typed IR.} Nodes carry type information (e.g., \texttt{Number}, \texttt{String}) that enables type-specialized code generation.
\end{itemize}

