\section{Graph Maintenance and Incremental Updates}\label{sec:graph-maintenance}

The sea of nodes requires careful maintenance of the graph structure as optimizations are applied. Each transformation must preserve the graph's invariants.

\subsection{Graph Invariants}\label{sec:graph-invariants}

The sea of nodes maintains several invariants:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Dominance.} Each node is dominated by its control inputs. A node can only execute in a region that is dominated by its control predecessor.
  \item \textbf{Single use of phi inputs.} Each phi node's input from a given predecessor is used exactly once.
  \item \textbf{Type consistency.} Each node's inputs have types compatible with the node's requirements.
  \item \textbf{No cycles (except loops).} The data dependency graph is acyclic, except for loop-carried dependencies through phi nodes.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Node Replacement}\label{sec:node-replacement}

When a node is optimized (e.g., constant-folded), it is replaced by its result. The replacement must update all uses of the old node:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Node Replacement in Sea of Nodes}\label{alg:node-replacement}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{Replace}{$old, new$}
  \For{each use $u$ of $old$}
    \State Replace the input of $u$ that references $old$ with $new$
  \EndFor
  \If{$old$ has no remaining uses}
    \State Remove $old$ from the graph
    \State \Comment{Recursively remove dead nodes}
  \EndIf
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Peephole Optimization on Graphs}\label{sec:graph-peephole}

Graph peephole optimizations match patterns in the graph and replace them with simpler equivalents:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Pattern: add(x, 0) -> x
  if (node is Add && node.right is Const(0)) {
    replace(node, node.left);
  }

  // Pattern: mul(x, 1) -> x
  if (node is Mul && node.right is Const(1)) {
    replace(node, node.left);
  }

  // Pattern: load(store(addr, val), addr) -> val (if no aliasing)
  if (node is Load && node.input is Store && node.addr == store.addr) {
    replace(node, store.value);
  }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Iterative Optimization}\label{sec:iterative-optimization}

Optimizations are applied iteratively until a fixed point is reached:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Iterative Graph Optimization}\label{alg:iterative-opt}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{OptimizeGraph}{$G$}
  \Repeat
    \State $\mathrm{changed} \gets \mathrm{false}$
    \For{each node $n$ in $G$}
      \State $\mathrm{result} \gets \mathrm{try\_optimize}(n)$
      \If{$\mathrm{result} \neq \mathrm{none}$}
        \State $\mathrm{Replace}(n, \mathrm{result})$
        \State $\mathrm{changed} \gets \mathrm{true}$
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
  \Until{$\neg \mathrm{changed}$}
  \State \Return $G$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

The iterative approach ensures that optimizations cascade: an early optimization may enable a later one that was not applicable before.

\subsection{Worklist-Based Optimization}\label{sec:worklist-optimization}

A more efficient approach uses a worklist to track nodes that need re-examination:

\begin{itemize}
  \item When a node is modified, add its uses to the worklist.
  \item Process nodes from the worklist until it is empty.
  \item This avoids re-examining nodes that have not changed.
\end{itemize}

