\section{Comparison with SSA-Based IRs}\label{sec:sea-vs-ssa}

The sea of nodes and SSA-based IRs represent two different approaches to the same problem.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Sea of nodes vs. SSA-based IRs}\label{tab:sea-vs-ssa}
\begin{tabular}{p{3cm}p{5cm}p{5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{Sea of Nodes} & \textbf{SSA-Based IR} \\
\hline
Structure & Unordered graph & Linear blocks + phi functions \\
Scheduling & Deferred until code generation & Fixed by block ordering \\
Optimization style & Graph rewriting & Pattern matching on linear code \\
Global optimizations & Natural (GVN, PRE) & Require dataflow analysis \\
Complexity & Higher (graph maintenance) & Lower (linear traversal) \\
Adoption & JIT compilers (Graal, V8) & Static compilers (LLVM, GCC) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The sea of nodes excels at global optimizations because it represents the program as a single graph, free from the artificial constraints of block ordering. SSA-based IRs are simpler to implement and reason about, making them preferred for static compilation.

\begin{insight}
The choice between sea-of-nodes and SSA-based IRs often reflects the compilation context. JIT compilers, which must optimize quickly and aggressively, benefit from the sea of nodes' ability to perform global optimizations in a single pass. Static compilers, which can afford multiple optimization passes, prefer the simplicity and predictability of SSA-based IRs. The trend in modern compilers is toward hybrid approaches that combine the strengths of both.
\end{insight}

