\section{Advanced Scheduling Algorithms}\label{sec:advanced-scheduling}

Scheduling in the sea of nodes is the process of assigning each floating node to a concrete position in a linear instruction sequence while respecting data and control dependencies. The quality of the schedule directly affects the performance of the generated code: a good schedule minimizes register pressure, maximizes instruction-level parallelism, and respects the processor's resource constraints.

\subsection{Earliest--Latest Scheduling Bounds}\label{sec:earliest-latest}

For each node $n$ in the sea of nodes, we define two bounds on its legal placement:

\begin{definition}[Scheduling Bounds]\label{def:sched-bounds}
Given a sea-of-nodes graph $G$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The \emph{earliest legal position} of $n$, denoted $\mathrm{earliest}(n)$, is the earliest region in the dominator tree where $n$ can be placed such that all of $n$'s inputs are available.
  \item The \emph{latest legal position} of $n$, denoted $\mathrm{latest}(n)$, is the latest region where $n$ can be placed such that all of $n$'s uses are dominated by $n$'s placement.
\end{itemize}
The \emph{scheduling range} of $n$ is the interval $[\mathrm{earliest}(n), \mathrm{latest}(n)]$ in the dominator tree.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Compute Earliest Position}\label{alg:earliest}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{ComputeEarliest}{$n$}
  \If{$n$ is a control node (Region, If, Loop)}
    \State \Return the region of $n$'s control input
  \EndIf
  \State $e \gets \mathrm{Start}$ \Comment{The entry region}
  \For{each data input $d$ of $n$}
    \State $e_d \gets \mathrm{region}(\mathrm{def}(d))$
    \State $e \gets \mathrm{LCA}(e, e_d)$ \Comment{Lowest common ancestor in dominator tree}
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $e$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

The latest position is computed symmetrically by walking upward from the uses:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Compute Latest Position}\label{alg:latest}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{ComputeLatest}{$n$}
  \If{$n$ has no uses}
    \State \Return the region of $n$'s control input
  \EndIf
  \State $l \gets \mathrm{region}(\mathrm{first\_use}(n))$
  \For{each use $u$ of $n$}
    \State $r_u \gets \mathrm{region}(u)$
    \State $l \gets \mathrm{LCA}(l, r_u)$
  \EndFor
  \State \Return $l$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Verifying a Local Schedule Against Dependency Edges}

Once graph scheduling places operations into blocks, the linearizer
can check each emitted block against the edges that constrain its
local order. This is cheaper than rerunning the scheduler and
helps distinguish a placement error from a bad local permutation.
Assume the input includes the assigned schedulable nodes and all
required local precedence edges, including sequencing edges for
effects. Phi values and values arriving from other blocks are
available on entry under a separate placement check. The verifier
does not try to infer a missing dependency from opcode names.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Assigned nodes $N$, local edges $E$, proposed list $L$
\State $p\gets$ empty position map
\For{$i=0$ to $|L|-1$}
  \State $v\gets L[i]$
  \If{$v\notin N$ or $v$ already has a position}
    \State \Return invalid node $v$ \Comment{Order must be a permutation}
  \EndIf
  \State $p[v]\gets i$
\EndFor
\If{$|p|\ne|N|$}
  \State \Return a missing node
\EndIf
\ForAll{$(u,v)\in E$}
  \If{$p[u]\ge p[v]$}
    \State \Return violated edge $(u,v)$ \Comment{Producer must precede user}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return valid local order
\end{sudocode}

Suppose node 11 computes an address, node 14 stores a value
there, and node 18 loads the same address. The graph contains
$11\to14$ and $11\to18$ as data edges, plus $14\to18$ as
a memory edge. Schedule $[11,18,14]$ respects both data edges
but fails the memory check, since position 2 is not before
position 1. Schedule $[11,14,18]$ passes. A schedule that
duplicates node 14 is rejected even if its last occurrence
happens to satisfy the edge tests.

\Cref{fig:visual-57} shows the edge that rejects the bad order even
though both uses of the address have their input available.

