After the first loop, the position map is a bijection from
assigned nodes to output positions. The second loop then checks
exactly the supplied order constraints in $O(|N|+|E|)$ time
and $O(|N|)$ space. Cycles among local precedence edges will
prevent every permutation from passing; loop-carried dependencies
must be represented at block boundaries rather than as edges in
this list. Target issue slots and latencies require extra checks
if the schedule also promises a particular cycle assignment.

\subsection{Global Code Motion in Detail}\label{sec:gcm-detail}

Click's global code motion algorithm~\cite{click1995} proceeds in three phases:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Phase 1: Compute earliest positions.} For each node, compute the earliest region where it can be legally placed. This is done bottom-up in the dominator tree.
  \item \textbf{Phase 2: Compute latest positions.} For each node, compute the latest region. This is done top-down.
  \item \textbf{Phase 3: Select positions.} For each node, choose a position within $[\mathrm{earliest}, \mathrm{latest}]$ using a heuristic.
\end{enumerate}

The position selection heuristic can optimize for different objectives:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Scheduling heuristics and their objectives}
\label{tab:sched-heuristics}
\begin{tabular}{lp{5cm}p{4cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Heuristic} & \textbf{Strategy} & \textbf{Objective} \\
\hline
Earliest & Place at $\mathrm{earliest}(n)$ & Maximize parallelism \\
Latest & Place at $\mathrm{latest}(n)$ & Minimize register pressure \\
Balanced & Place at midpoint & Trade-off \\
Speculative & Place before guard & Enable speculative execution \\
Profile-guided & Use execution frequency & Minimize expected cost \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Schedule Selection with Cost Models}\label{sec:cost-models}

Modern schedulers use cost models to select the best position for each node:

\begin{definition}[Schedule Cost Model]\label{def:cost-model}
A \emph{schedule cost model} assigns a cost to each legal placement of a node. The total schedule cost is the sum of individual node costs:
\[
  C(S) = \sum_{n \in G} c(n, \mathrm{pos}(n))
\]
where $c(n, p)$ is the cost of placing node $n$ at position $p$.
\end{definition}

Cost factors include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Register pressure.} Placing a node far from its uses increases the number of simultaneously live values, increasing register pressure.
  \item \textbf{Loop-invariant code motion.} Placing a node outside a loop (when possible) reduces execution frequency.
  \item \textbf{Speculation.} Placing a node before a guard enables speculative execution but requires deoptimization on guard failure.
  \item \textbf{Instruction latency.} Placing a node early allows its result to be ready when needed, hiding latency.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Trace Scheduling}\label{sec:trace-scheduling}

\emph{Trace scheduling} is a technique for scheduling the sea of nodes along the most likely execution path (the \emph{hot trace}):

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Identify the hot trace (the most frequently executed path through the graph).
  \item Schedule nodes along the trace first, optimizing for the trace's execution.
  \item For nodes not on the trace, insert compensation code at the trace's entry and exit points.
  \item Schedule the remaining nodes for the cold paths.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Hot trace: entry -> A -> B -> C -> exit
  // Cold path: entry -> A -> D -> exit
  
  // Schedule for hot trace:
  //   [entry] -> [A] -> [B] -> [C] -> [exit]
  //   (nodes scheduled to minimize latency on this path)
  
  // Compensation at D:
  //   [D] needs a value computed in B (on hot trace)
  //   Insert compensation code: recompute the value
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Software Pipelining from the Sea of Nodes}\label{sec:software-pipelining}

The sea of nodes enables \emph{software pipelining}: overlapping iterations of a loop to hide latencies. The scheduler can:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Identify loop-carried dependencies through phi nodes.
  \item Determine the minimum initiation interval (MII) based on resource and recurrence constraints.
  \item Schedule operations from different iterations in the same loop body.
  \item Generate prologue and epilogue code for the pipelined loop.
\end{itemize}

