%% Section 57.10 — Phi Node Handling in Depth
%% ============================================================
\section{Phi Node Handling in Depth}\label{sec:phi-depth}

Phi nodes are the most subtle aspect of the sea of nodes. They represent the merging of data values at control-flow join points and must be handled carefully during optimization and scheduling.

\subsection{Phi Node Classification}\label{sec:phi-classification}

Phi nodes in the sea of nodes can be classified by their role:

\begin{definition}[Phi Node Types]\label{def:phi-types}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Loop phi}: a phi node whose region is a loop header. One input comes from outside the loop (the initial value), and the other from inside the loop (the loop-carried value).
  \item \emph{Join phi}: a phi node at a control-flow merge point (if-then-else). Each input comes from a different branch.
  \item \emph{Trivial phi}: a phi node where all inputs are the same value. Can be replaced by that value.
  \item \emph{Copy phi}: a phi node where one input is the phi itself (a self-loop). Represents a variable that is unchanged on one path.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Phi Node Optimization Rules}\label{sec:phi-rules}

The following optimization rules apply to phi nodes in the sea of nodes:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Phi node optimization rules}
\label{tab:phi-rules}
\begin{tabular}{lp{5cm}p{4cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Rule} & \textbf{Pattern} & \textbf{Replacement} \\
\hline
Identity & $\phi(r, v, v, \ldots, v)$ & $v$ \\
Copy & $\phi(r, v, \phi(r, v, \ldots))$ & $v$ \\
Cycle & $\phi(r, v, \phi(r, \ldots, \phi))$ & Loop-carried value analysis \\
Unused & $\phi$ with no data uses & Remove (if region is live) \\
Redundant & $\phi_1 = \phi_2$ (same inputs) & Merge $\phi_1$ and $\phi_2$ \\
Hoist & $\phi$ with loop-invariant inputs & Move phi before the loop \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Phi Resolution During Scheduling}\label{sec:phi-resolution}

When the sea of nodes is scheduled into basic blocks, phi nodes must be resolved into copy instructions. This is the \emph{copy coalescing} problem:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item For each phi node $\phi(r, v_1, v_2)$ in region $r$:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Insert a copy of $v_1$ to the phi's destination at the end of predecessor 1.
    \item Insert a copy of $v_2$ to the phi's destination at the end of predecessor 2.
  \end{itemize}
  \item Use register allocation to coalesce the copies with the phi's destination, eliminating moves where possible.
  \item Handle the \emph{swap problem}: when two phi nodes exchange values, insert swap sequences using temporary registers.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Before scheduling:
  // Region R:
  //   x = phi(R, a, b)    // x = a from pred1, b from pred2
  //   y = phi(R, b, a)    // y = b from pred1, a from pred2
  
  // After scheduling (with copies):
  // pred1:
  //   tmp = x; x = a; y = tmp   // swap needed!
  // pred2:
  //   tmp = x; x = b; y = tmp   // swap needed!
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Parallel Copy Resolution}\label{sec:parallel-copy}

The phi resolution problem is equivalent to decomposing a \emph{parallel copy} into a sequence of sequential copies:

\begin{definition}[Parallel Copy]\label{def:parallel-copy}
A \emph{parallel copy} is a simultaneous assignment $(d_1, \ldots, d_n) \leftarrow (s_1, \ldots, s_n)$ where all reads happen before any writes. It must be decomposed into sequential copies using at most one temporary register.
\end{definition}

The decomposition algorithm constructs a \emph{copy graph} where each edge $s_i \to d_i$ represents a required copy. The graph decomposes into:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Trees} (acyclic components): copies can be sequenced in topological order.
  \item \textbf{Cycles}: require a temporary register to break the cycle.
\end{itemize}

