%% Section 57.11 — Detailed Comparison with SSA
%% ============================================================
\section{Detailed Comparison with SSA-Based IRs}\label{sec:detailed-ssa-comparison}

While the sea of nodes and SSA-based IRs are often presented as alternatives, they share deep structural similarities. Understanding these similarities and differences is essential for compiler designers.

\subsection{Structural Correspondence}\label{sec:structural-correspondence}

\begin{theorem}[Sea-of-Nodes / SSA Correspondence]\label{thm:sea-ssa}
Every sea-of-nodes graph can be converted to an equivalent SSA-form program by scheduling the graph into basic blocks and inserting phi nodes at join points. Conversely, every SSA-form program can be represented as a sea-of-nodes graph by ``floating'' the instructions out of their blocks.
\end{theorem}

The conversion from sea of nodes to SSA proceeds as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Schedule each node to a basic block.
  \item For each phi node in the sea of nodes, generate a corresponding SSA phi function.
  \item For each data edge crossing a block boundary, ensure the value is available (possibly through an SSA phi).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Optimization Power Comparison}\label{sec:opt-power}

The key difference between the two representations lies in their optimization power:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Optimization capabilities: sea of nodes vs.\ SSA}
\label{tab:opt-power}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Optimization} & \textbf{Sea of Nodes} & \textbf{SSA IR} \\
\hline
Global value numbering & Natural (node identity) & Requires hash-based value numbering \\
Partial redundancy elim. & Lazy code motion (automatic) & Requires PRE algorithm \\
Loop-invariant code motion & Floating (automatic) & Requires LICM pass \\
Speculative optimization & Guard nodes (natural) & Requires explicit checks \\
Dead code elimination & Reachability (trivial) & Requires liveness analysis \\
Common subexpression elim. & Node sharing (automatic) & Requires CSE pass \\
Strength reduction & Graph rewriting & Requires pattern matching \\
Induction variable elim. & Phi analysis & Requires IV recognition \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Hybrid Approaches}\label{sec:hybrid-approaches}

Several modern compilers use hybrid approaches that combine elements of both:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{LLVM with sea-of-nodes extensions.} Some LLVM passes (e.g., NewGVN) use graph-based reasoning on top of the linear SSA IR.
  \item \textbf{Graal's schedule-late approach.} Graal keeps nodes floating until late in the pipeline, then schedules them into a CFG for the final code generation.
  \item \textbf{V8's Maglev.} V8's mid-tier compiler uses a CFG-based IR but with sea-of-nodes-inspired optimizations.
  \item \textbf{Swift's SIL.} Swift Intermediate Language uses a hybrid representation with both SSA values and instruction-level operations.
\end{itemize}

\begin{insight}
The fundamental insight is that the sea of nodes and SSA are not truly different representations---they are different points on a spectrum of ``floatingness.'' In SSA, instructions are fixed to basic blocks; in the sea of nodes, they float freely. The optimal point on this spectrum depends on the compilation context: JIT compilers benefit from maximum floatingness (to enable aggressive optimization in a single pass), while static compilers benefit from partial floatingness (to enable incremental optimization across multiple passes).
\end{insight}

\subsection{Converting Between Representations}\label{sec:converting-reps}

The conversion between sea of nodes and SSA is not merely theoretical---it occurs in practice at the boundary between optimization and code generation:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Sea of Nodes to SSA Conversion}\label{alg:sea-to-ssa}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{SeaOfNodesToSSA}{$G$}
  \State Schedule all nodes to basic blocks (Section~\ref{sec:scheduling})
  \State Build the CFG from the scheduled blocks
  \State Compute dominance frontiers
  \For{each data edge $(u, v)$ crossing a block boundary}
    \If{$u$'s block does not dominate $v$'s block}
      \State Insert SSA phi functions at dominance frontiers
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State Rename variables to SSA form
  \State \Return the SSA program
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

