%% Section 57.12
%% ============================================================
\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-57}

Graph-based IRs, particularly the sea of nodes, represent a fundamentally different approach to intermediate representation. By abandoning the linear instruction sequence in favor of an unordered graph, they enable optimizations that are difficult or impossible in linear IRs. The cost is complexity: the graph must be maintained, scheduled, and lowered to a linear form before code generation.

Graph maintenance requires careful attention to invariants (dominance, type consistency, acyclicity). Iterative optimization with worklists enables cascading optimizations. The comparison with SSA-based IRs reveals a fundamental trade-off between optimization power and implementation simplicity.

For further reading, we recommend Click's thesis~\cite{click1995}, the Graal compiler documentation~\cite{graal2024}, and the V8 TurboFan design document~\cite{turbofan2024}. The paper by Click and Cooper~\cite{click1995b} on global code motion provides the algorithmic foundation for scheduling in sea-of-nodes IRs.
